port\n\n### Naming Evaluation (FIRST PRIORITY)\n\nReviewed all function/variable names against their current, final-state purpose:\n\n- `runScenario`, `computeQuotePremium`, `computeClaimResult`, `processClaimStep`, `basePremiumFor`, `insuranceValueFor`, `lookupItemTypeValue`, `isComponentBlockOfThree`, `sumBasePremiums`, `itemSurcharge`, `isLoyalCustomer`, `payoutForDamage`, `reimbursementRate`, `policyCap`, `countByKey`, `validateDamagesAgainstPolicy` — all still accurately describe what they do.\n- `quoteStepsSeen` (the new counter from the last Green phase) was the one candidate for improvement.\n\n**Assessment**: `quoteStepsSeen` is a counter (`let ... += 1`) used only to answer a yes/no question (\"has a quote happened before?\"). The name describes *how* the value is produced (a running count) rather than *why* it exists (to know if this is the first contract). This is exactly the kind of implementation-detail name Rule 2 flags.\n\n**Decision**: Renamed `quoteStepsSeen: number` (mutated via `+= 1`) to `hasQuotedBefore: boolean` (mutated via `= true`). This directly states the fact the code cares about, eliminates the indirection of comparing a counter to `0`, and removes the unnecessary increment-by-one arithmetic. Also added a clarifying comment on `runScenario` explaining why only the *first* quote step counts as \"first contract\" (this was implicit domain knowledge worth documenting since no more tests will pin it down further).\n\n### Initial APP Mass (relevant excerpt — `runScenario`)\n\n```js\nexport function runScenario(scenario: Scenario): ScenarioResult {\n  const capUsedByPolicyIndex = new Map<number, number>();\n  let quoteStepsSeen = 0;\n  const results: StepResult[] = scenario.steps.map((step) => {\n    if (step.op === \"quote\") {\n      const isFirstContract = quoteStepsSeen === 0;\n      quoteStepsSeen += 1;\n      return { premium: computeQuotePremium(scenario.customer, step.items, isFirstContract) };\n    }\n    return processClaimStep(step